P10("p12-heart-and-brain-models","Practical 12: Models of the human heart and brain",
 "Look inside a model heart and brain and label what you see.",
 "The aim is to observe and draw labelled sketches of the longitudinal section of the human heart and brain from models.",
 "The heart has four chambers: two auricles and two ventricles separated by septa which prevent mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. The tricuspid valve lies between the right auricle and ventricle and the bicuspid valve between the left auricle and ventricle. The heart is covered by the pericardium.",
 "The brain lies in the cranial cavity and controls the body. It is covered by three meninges (dura mater, arachnoid and pia mater) and has forebrain, midbrain and hindbrain.",
 ["auricle","ventricle","pericardium","meninges","cerebrum"],
 ["Observe the heart model and count chambers.","Name valves and membranes.","Observe the brain and its parts."],
 ("The heart has two chambers.",["It has four.","Two auricles and two ventricles."]),
 ["m|Number of heart chambers|4|2|3|Two auricles and two ventricles.","m|Valve between left auricle and ventricle|bicuspid|tricuspid|aortic|Bicuspid.","t|The brain is covered by meninges.|T|Yes.","m|The heart's covering is the|pericardium|pleura|skull|Pericardium.","s|How many parts does the brain have (fore, mid, hind)?|three;3|Three parts.","t|The tricuspid valve is on the right side.|T|Yes."])
P10("p13-blood-cells","Practical 13: Identification of blood cells",
 "Red cells carry oxygen and white cells guard the body.",
 "The aim is to identify red and white blood cells from permanent slides, draw labelled diagrams and write notes.",
 "Red blood cells (erythrocytes) are biconcave discs; mature mammalian RBCs have no nucleus and contain haemoglobin, which gives the red colour and transports oxygen and carbon dioxide.",
 "White blood cells (leucocytes) are colourless and have a nucleus. They defend the body against germs. Draw both types with labels.",
 ["erythrocyte","leucocyte","haemoglobin","biconcave","nucleus"],
 ["Focus the slide.","Identify RBC or WBC by shape and nucleus.","Draw and write a note."],
 ("RBCs have a nucleus.",["Mature mammalian RBCs do not.","WBCs have a nucleus."]),
 ["m|Haemoglobin gives|red colour|white colour|blue colour|It carries oxygen.","m|Another name for RBC|erythrocyte|leucocyte|platelet|Erythrocyte.","t|WBCs are colourless.|T|Yes.","m|RBC shape|biconcave disc|round ball|star|Biconcave.","s|What do RBCs carry?|oxygen|Oxygen.","t|Mature mammalian RBCs have a nucleus.|F|They do not."])
P10("p14-endocrine-glands","Practical 14: Identification of endocrine glands",
 "Two small glands, the thyroid and the pancreas, control your metabolism and blood sugar.",
 "The aim is to identify the thyroid gland and the pancreas (islets of Langerhans) from a model or chart and write their location, hormones and functions.",
 "The thyroid is a bilobed gland in the neck on either side of the trachea. It secretes triiodothyronine (T3) and thyroxine (T4), which raise the metabolic rate and regulate growth. Lack of thyroxine causes goitre, myxoedema and cretinism, and excess causes Graves' disease.",
 "The islets of Langerhans in the pancreas secrete insulin from beta cells and glucagon from alpha cells. Insulin converts glucose to glycogen; glucagon converts glycogen to glucose, and they keep the blood sugar at 80 to 120 mg/dl. Less insulin causes diabetes mellitus.",
 ["thyroid","insulin","glucagon","hormone","goitre"],
 ["Identify the flagged gland.","Write location and hormones.","Write functions and disorders."],
 ("Insulin raises blood sugar.",["It lowers it.","Glucagon raises it."]),
 ["m|Insulin is secreted by|beta cells|alpha cells|thyroid|Beta cells.","m|Thyroxine deficiency can cause|goitre|diabetes|fever|Goitre.","t|The thyroid is in the neck.|T|Yes.","m|Glucagon converts|glycogen to glucose|glucose to glycogen|water to air|Raises sugar.","s|Low insulin causes which disease?|diabetes mellitus;diabetes|Diabetes mellitus.","t|Pancreas contains islets of Langerhans.|T|Yes."])
